    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════
    // CHALLENGE ROUND, 5 Questions
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════
    cq('You have a $100,000 capital gain and $80,000 in unrealized losses in your portfolio. After harvesting all losses, what is your net taxable gain, and approximately how much do you save in taxes at the 23.8% combined rate?',
      ['Net gain: $100K, savings: $0, because harvested losses can only offset future gains, not current ones','Net gain: $20K, savings: approximately $19,040 (23.8% on $80K in harvested losses)','Net gain: $0, savings: $23,800, since the $80K of losses wipes out the entire $100K gain to zero','Net gain: $80K, savings: $4,760, because losses can only offset 20% of a gain in any single tax year'],
      1,'The $80K in harvested losses offsets $80K of your $100K gain, leaving a net taxable gain of $20K. At the 23.8% combined rate (20% long-term capital gains + 3.8% NIIT), you save approximately $19,040. The $20K remaining gain would cost $4,760 in taxes, still far better than paying $23,800 on the full $100K.'),
    cq('Sarah has $500K in a Traditional IRA and expects minimal income during a 2-year early retirement gap before starting a new job. Her normal bracket is 32%. What Roth conversion strategy maximizes her tax savings?',
      ['Convert the entire $500K in year one, since one large conversion is taxed at a flat low rate instead of climbing the brackets','Convert approximately $200K-$250K each year to fill the 22-24% brackets, saving roughly $40,000-$50,000 compared to paying 32% later','Skip the conversion entirely, since Traditional IRA withdrawals are always taxed at a lower rate once you are officially retired','Wait until age 73 when required minimum distributions begin, because converting before RMDs start triggers an early withdrawal penalty'],
      1,'By spreading $500K in conversions across 2 low-income years, Sarah fills the 22-24% brackets each year. The average rate is roughly 22% instead of the 32% she\'d pay on withdrawals later. On $500K, the difference between 22% and 32% is $50,000 in total tax savings. This is one of the most powerful legal tax strategies available, it just requires planning.'),
    cq('Why does donating appreciated stock to charity save MORE in taxes than donating the same dollar amount in cash?',
      ['It does not save more, both produce the exact same tax result, so a donor should simply give whichever asset is most convenient','Donating stock earns a larger charitable deduction than the same dollar amount of cash, which is what creates the extra savings','Both produce the same charitable deduction, but donating stock ALSO eliminates the capital gains tax on the appreciated amount that you would owe if you sold the stock first','The annual limit on stock donations is higher than on cash, so you can deduct a bigger gift in a single year by giving stock'],
      2,'Both a $50K cash donation and a $50K stock donation give you a $50K charitable deduction. But if that stock has a $40K unrealized gain, donating it directly eliminates the $8,000-$9,520 in capital gains tax you\'d owe if you sold first. The charity receives the same $50K value, you get the same deduction, but you save an extra $8K-$9.5K. It\'s strictly better to donate appreciated stock.'),
    cq('The "Buy, Borrow, Die" strategy lets billionaires avoid income tax. Which step PERMANENTLY eliminates capital gains tax?',
      ['Buy, because the act of purchasing an asset locks in its cost basis and shields all later gains from any future taxation','Borrow, because taking a loan against an asset permanently cancels the capital gains tax owed on that underlying asset','Die, the step-up in basis at death resets the cost basis to current fair market value, permanently erasing all unrealized gains','None of the three steps actually eliminate the tax, they only push it into the future, so the gain is always paid eventually'],
      2,'The step-up in basis at death is the most powerful tax provision in the entire US tax code. When the asset holder dies, the cost basis of all inherited assets resets to the current fair market value. A stock bought at $1 and worth $1,000 at death has its basis reset to $1,000. The $999 gain is permanently erased, not deferred, not transferred, but eliminated from the tax code entirely.'),
    cq('An investor with a $1M portfolio using automated tax-loss harvesting at 1.8% annual tax alpha versus an identical portfolio without harvesting, approximately how much more wealth does the harvesting portfolio have after 25 years (assuming 8% base return)?',
      ['About $10,000 more, because the 1.8% tax saving applies only once at the very end of the 25-year holding period','About $50,000 more, found by simply multiplying the $1M starting balance by the 1.8% annual tax alpha figure','About $200,000-$350,000 more due to compounding of the annual 1.8% tax savings','The same amount, because tax savings are spent each year and never get reinvested to compound alongside the portfolio'],
      2,'The 1.8% annual tax alpha doesn\'t just add linearly, it compounds. A $1M portfolio at 8% grows to $6.85M over 25 years. The same portfolio at 9.8% (8% + 1.8% tax alpha) grows to $10.2M, a difference of approximately $3.35M. Even at a more conservative calculation where only the harvesting savings are reinvested separately, the difference is $200K-$350K+. This is why automated tax-loss harvesting is one of the highest-return activities in all of personal finance.')
