      {label:'Switch core portfolio management to a robo-advisor (0.25% = $500/year) for automated rebalancing, daily TLH, and diversified portfolio construction. The $1,500/year savings gets reinvested. For complex needs (tax planning, estate planning), consult a fee-only financial planner annually ($1,500-$2,500 flat fee). Total cost: $2,000-$3,000/year with better daily portfolio management than the 1% advisor provides. Over 30 years: the $1,500/year in fee savings (reinvested at 7%) compounds to $142K+ in additional wealth.',outcome:'Optimal hybrid approach. The robo-advisor handles the daily work (rebalancing, TLH) better than a human can, algorithms don\'t forget, don\'t get emotional, and check positions every day. The fee-only planner provides human judgment for complex decisions. The cost is similar or lower than the 1% advisor, but the quality of daily portfolio management is higher.',correct:true},
      {label:'Stay with the 1% advisor, you get what you pay for',outcome:'Not necessarily. Research shows that the median financial advisor does not add enough alpha to justify a 1% fee after accounting for tax inefficiency and missed TLH opportunities. Robo-advisors provide superior daily portfolio management (daily TLH, continuous rebalancing) at 75% lower cost. The 1% fee makes sense only if the advisor provides exceptional planning, tax, and behavioral coaching value.',correct:false},
      {label:'Manage everything yourself using free brokerage accounts',outcome:'DIY investing avoids advisory fees entirely but requires significant time and discipline. Most DIY investors fail to rebalance consistently, miss tax-loss harvesting opportunities, and make emotional decisions during market stress (selling low, buying high). A robo-advisor at 0.25% provides professional management at minimal cost, the fee is paid for many times over by TLH savings alone.',correct:false}
